              "kind": "definition",
              "section": "1.1",
              "subsection": "1.1.3",
              "rawText": "Deﬁnition 6 Let p and q be propositions. The biconditional statement p ↔ q is the proposition “p if and\nonly ifq.” The biconditional statementp ↔ q is true whenp and q have the same truth values,\nand is false otherwise. Biconditional statements are also called bi-implications.",
              "text": "Definition 6 Let p and q be propositions. The biconditional statement p ↔ q is the proposition “p if and only ifq.” The biconditional statementp ↔ q is true whenp and q have the same truth values, and is false otherwise. Biconditional statements are also called bi-implications.",
