              "title": "Let p be the statement “You can take the flight,” and let q be the statement “You buy a ticket.”",
              "hasSolution": false,
              "promptRaw": "Let p be the statement “You can take the ﬂight,” and let q be the statement “You buy a ticket.”\nThen p ↔ q is the statement\n“You can take the ﬂight if and only if you buy a ticket.”\nThis statement is true if p and q are either both true or both false, that is, if you buy a ticket and\nExtra \nExamples\ncan take the ﬂight or if you do not buy a ticket and you cannot take the ﬂight. It is false when\np and q have opposite truth values, that is, when you do not buy a ticket, but you can take the\nﬂight (such as when you get a free trip) and when you buy a ticket but you cannot take the ﬂight\n(such as when the airline bumps you).\n◂",
              "prompt": "Let p be the statement “You can take the flight,” and let q be the statement “You buy a ticket.” Then p ↔ q is the statement “You can take the flight if and only if you buy a ticket.” This statement is true if p and q are either both true or both false, that is, if you buy a ticket and Extra Examples can take the flight or if you do not buy a ticket and you cannot take the flight. It is false when p and q have opposite truth values, that is, when you do not buy a ticket, but you can take the flight (such as when you get a free trip) and when you buy a ticket but you cannot take the flight (such as when the airline bumps you). ◂",
