              "rawText": "We pose more of Smullyan’s puzzles about knights and knaves in Exercises 23–27. In Ex-\nercises 28–35 we introduce related puzzles where we have three types of people, knights and\nknaves as in this puzzle together with spies who can lie.\nNext, we pose a puzzle known as themuddy children puzzle for the case of two children.",
              "text": "We pose more of Smullyan’s puzzles about knights and knaves in Exercises 23–27. In Exercises 28–35 we introduce related puzzles where we have three types of people, knights and knaves as in this puzzle together with spies who can lie. Next, we pose a puzzle known as themuddy children puzzle for the case of two children.",
