              "promptRaw": "Show that (p∧q) → (p∨q) is a tautology.\nSolution: To show that this statement is a tautology, we will use logical equivalences to demon-",
              "prompt": "Show that (p∧q) → (p∨q) is a tautology. Solution: To show that this statement is a tautology, we will use logical equivalences to demon-",
              "solutionRaw": "To show that this statement is a tautology, we will use logical equivalences to demon-\nstrate that it is logically equivalent to T.( Note: This could also be done using a truth table.)\n(p∧q) → (p∨q) ≡ ¬(p∧q)∨(p∨q) by Example 3\n≡ (¬p∨¬q)∨(p∨q) b yt h eﬁ r s tD eM o r g a nl a w\n≡ (¬p∨p)∨(¬q∨q) by the associative and commutative\nlaws for disjunction\n≡ T∨T by Example 1 and the commutative\nlaw for disjunction\n≡ T by the domination law ◂",
              "solution": "To show that this statement is a tautology, we will use logical equivalences to demonstrate that it is logically equivalent to T.( Note: This could also be done using a truth table.) (p∧q) → (p∨q) ≡ ¬(p∧q)∨(p∨q) by Example 3 ≡ (¬p∨¬q)∨(p∨q) b yt h efi r s tD eM o r g a nl a w ≡ (¬p∨p)∨(¬q∨q) by the associative and commutative laws for disjunction ≡ T∨T by Example 1 and the commutative law for disjunction ≡ T by the domination law ◂",
