              "section": "1.4",
              "subsection": "1.4.2",
              "rawText": "In general, a statement involving the n variables x1,x 2, … ,x n can be denoted by\nP(x1,x 2, … ,x n).\nA statement of the form P(x1,x 2, … ,x n) is the value of the propositional function P at the\nn-tuple (x1,x 2, … ,x n), and P is also called an n-place predicate or an n-ary predicate.\nPropositional functions occur in computer programs, as Example 6 demonstrates.",
              "text": "In general, a statement involving the n variables x1,x 2, … ,x n can be denoted by P(x1,x 2, … ,x n). A statement of the form P(x1,x 2, … ,x n) is the value of the propositional function P at the n-tuple (x1,x 2, … ,x n), and P is also called an n-place predicate or an n-ary predicate. Propositional functions occur in computer programs, as Example 6 demonstrates.",
