              "kind": "example",
              "section": "1.4",
              "subsection": "1.4.4",
              "rawText": "EXAMPLE 16 What is the truth value of ∃xP(x), where P(x) is the statement “x 2 > 10” and the universe of\ndiscourse consists of the positive integers not exceeding 4?\nSolution: Because the domain is{1, 2, 3, 4}, the proposition∃xP(x) is the same as the disjunction\nP(1)∨P(2)∨P(3)∨P(4).\nBecause P(4), which is the statement “4 2 > 10,” is true, it follows that∃xP(x) is true. ◂",
              "text": "EXAMPLE 16 What is the truth value of ∃xP(x), where P(x) is the statement “x 2 > 10” and the universe of discourse consists of the positive integers not exceeding 4? Solution: Because the domain is{1, 2, 3, 4}, the proposition∃xP(x) is the same as the disjunction P(1)∨P(2)∨P(3)∨P(4). Because P(4), which is the statement “4 2 > 10,” is true, it follows that∃xP(x) is true. ◂",
