              "kind": "text",
              "section": "1.4",
              "subsection": "1.4.5",
              "rawText": "Note that the restriction of a universal quantiﬁcation is the same as the universal quantiﬁ-\ncation of a conditional statement. For instance, ∀x< 0( x2 > 0) is another way of expressing\n∀x(x < 0 → x2 > 0). On the other hand, the restriction of an existential quantiﬁcation is the\nsame as the existential quantiﬁcation of a conjunction. For instance, ∃z> 0( z2 = 2) is another\nway of expressing∃z(z> 0∧z2 = 2).",
              "text": "Note that the restriction of a universal quantification is the same as the universal quantification of a conditional statement. For instance, ∀x< 0( x2 > 0) is another way of expressing ∀x(x < 0 → x2 > 0). On the other hand, the restriction of an existential quantification is the same as the existential quantification of a conjunction. For instance, ∃z> 0( z2 = 2) is another way of expressing∃z(z> 0∧z2 = 2).",
