              "kind": "text",
              "section": "1.7",
              "subsection": "1.7.6",
              "rawText": "We can also quickly prove a conditional statement p → q if we know that the conclusion\nq is true. By showing that q is true, it follows that p → q must also be true. A proof of p → q\nthat uses the fact that q is true is called a trivial proof. Trivial proofs are often important when\nspecial cases of theorems are proved (see the discussion of proof by cases in Section 1.8) and\nin mathematical induction, which is a proof technique discussed in Section 5.1.",
              "text": "We can also quickly prove a conditional statement p → q if we know that the conclusion q is true. By showing that q is true, it follows that p → q must also be true. A proof of p → q that uses the fact that q is true is called a trivial proof. Trivial proofs are often important when special cases of theorems are proved (see the discussion of proof by cases in Section 1.8) and in mathematical induction, which is a proof technique discussed in Section 5.1.",
