              "solutionRaw": "This theorem has the form “p if and only if q,” where p is “n is odd” and q is “n 2\nis odd.” (As usual, we do not explicitly deal with the universal quantiﬁcation.) To prove this\ntheorem, we need to show that p → q and q → p are true.\nWe have already shown (in Example 1) thatp → q is true and (in Example 8) that q → p is\nExtra \nExamples\ntrue.\nBecause we have shown that bothp → q and q → p are true, we have shown that the theorem\nis true. ◂",
              "solution": "This theorem has the form “p if and only if q,” where p is “n is odd” and q is “n 2 is odd.” (As usual, we do not explicitly deal with the universal quantification.) To prove this theorem, we need to show that p → q and q → p are true. We have already shown (in Example 1) thatp → q is true and (in Example 8) that q → p is Extra Examples true. Because we have shown that bothp → q and q → p are true, we have shown that the theorem is true. ◂",
