              "kind": "text",
              "section": "1.7",
              "subsection": "1.7.8",
              "rawText": "Finally, we brieﬂy discuss a particularly nasty type of error. Many incorrect arguments are\nbased on a fallacy called begging the question. This fallacy occurs when one or more steps of\na proof are based on the truth of the statement being proved. In other words, this fallacy arises\nwhen a statement is proved using itself, or a statement equivalent to it. That is why this fallacy\nis also called circular reasoning.",
              "text": "Finally, we briefly discuss a particularly nasty type of error. Many incorrect arguments are based on a fallacy called begging the question. This fallacy occurs when one or more steps of a proof are based on the truth of the statement being proved. In other words, this fallacy arises when a statement is proved using itself, or a statement equivalent to it. That is why this fallacy is also called circular reasoning.",
