              "rawText": "EXAMPLE 19 Is the following argument correct? It supposedly shows thatn is an even integer whenevern2 is\nan even integer.\nSuppose that n2 is even. Thenn2 = 2k for some integerk.L e tn= 2l for some integerl. This\nshows that n is even.\nSolution: This argument is incorrect. The statement “let n = 2l for some integerl” occurs in the\nproof. No argument has been given to show thatn can be written as 2lfor some integerl. This is\ncircular reasoning because this statement is equivalent to the statement being proved, namely,\n“n is even.” The result itself is correct; only the method of proof is wrong.\n◂",
