              "id": "rosen-ch1-text-6007-6012",
              "kind": "text",
              "section": "1.8",
              "subsection": "1.8.2",
              "rawText": "LEVERAGING PROOF BY CASES The examples we have presented illustrating proof by\ncases provide some insight into when to use this method of proof. In particular, when it is not\npossible to consider all cases of a proof at the same time, a proof by cases should be considered.\nWhen should you use such a proof? Generally, look for a proof by cases when there is no obvious\nway to begin a proof, but when extra information in each case helps move the proof forward.\nExample 5 illustrates how the method of proof by cases can be used eﬀectively.",
              "text": "LEVERAGING PROOF BY CASES The examples we have presented illustrating proof by cases provide some insight into when to use this method of proof. In particular, when it is not possible to consider all cases of a proof at the same time, a proof by cases should be considered. When should you use such a proof? Generally, look for a proof by cases when there is no obvious way to begin a proof, but when extra information in each case helps move the proof forward. Example 5 illustrates how the method of proof by cases can be used effectively.",
