              "promptRaw": "A Constructive Existence Proof Show that there is a positive integer that can be written as\nthe sum of cubes of positive integers in two diﬀerent ways.\nSolution: After considerable computation (such as a computer search) we ﬁnd that",
              "prompt": "A Constructive Existence Proof Show that there is a positive integer that can be written as the sum of cubes of positive integers in two different ways. Solution: After considerable computation (such as a computer search) we find that",
              "solutionRaw": "After considerable computation (such as a computer search) we ﬁnd that\nExtra \nExamples\n1729 = 103 + 93 = 123 + 13.\nBecause we have displayed a positive integer that can be written as the sum of cubes in two\ndiﬀerent ways, we are done.\nThere is an interesting story pertaining to this example. The English mathematician G. H.\nHardy, when visiting the ailing Indian prodigy Ramanujan in the hospital, remarked that 1729,\nthe number of the cab he took, was rather dull. Ramanujan replied “No, it is a very interesting\nnumber; it is the smallest number expressible as the sum of cubes in two diﬀerent ways.”\n◂",
              "solution": "After considerable computation (such as a computer search) we find that Extra Examples 1729 = 103 + 93 = 123 + 13. Because we have displayed a positive integer that can be written as the sum of cubes in two different ways, we are done. There is an interesting story pertaining to this example. The English mathematician G. H. Hardy, when visiting the ailing Indian prodigy Ramanujan in the hospital, remarked that 1729, the number of the cab he took, was rather dull. Ramanujan replied “No, it is a very interesting number; it is the smallest number expressible as the sum of cubes in two different ways.” ◂",
