              "promptRaw": "In Example 15 in Section 1.7 we showed that the statement “Every positive integer is the sum of\ntwo squares of integers” is false by ﬁnding a counterexample. That is, there are positive integers\nthat cannot be written as the sum of the squares of two integers. Although we cannot write every\npositive integer as the sum of the squares of two integers, maybe we can write every positive\ninteger as the sum of the squares of three integers. That is, is the statement “Every positive\ninteger is the sum of the squares of three integers” true or false?\nSolution: Because we know that not every positive integer can be written as the sum of two",
              "prompt": "In Example 15 in Section 1.7 we showed that the statement “Every positive integer is the sum of two squares of integers” is false by finding a counterexample. That is, there are positive integers that cannot be written as the sum of the squares of two integers. Although we cannot write every positive integer as the sum of the squares of two integers, maybe we can write every positive integer as the sum of the squares of three integers. That is, is the statement “Every positive integer is the sum of the squares of three integers” true or false? Solution: Because we know that not every positive integer can be written as the sum of two",
