              "kind": "text",
              "section": "1.8",
              "subsection": "1.8.9",
              "rawText": "This problem has a fascinating history. In the seventeenth century, Fermat jotted in the mar-\ngin of his copy of the works of Diophantus that he had a “wondrous proof” that there are no\ninteger solutions of x\nn + yn = zn when n is an integer greater than 2 with xyz ≠ 0. However,\nhe never published a proof (Fermat published almost nothing), and no proof could be found in\nthe papers he left when he died. Mathematicians looked for a proof for three centuries without\nsuccess, although many people were convinced that a relatively simple proof could be found.\n(Proofs of special cases were found, such as the proof of the case when n = 3 by Euler and\nthe proof of the n = 4 case by Fermat himself.) Over the years, several established mathemati-\ncians thought that they had proved this theorem. In the nineteenth century, one of these failed",
              "text": "This problem has a fascinating history. In the seventeenth century, Fermat jotted in the margin of his copy of the works of Diophantus that he had a “wondrous proof” that there are no integer solutions of x n + yn = zn when n is an integer greater than 2 with xyz ≠ 0. However, he never published a proof (Fermat published almost nothing), and no proof could be found in the papers he left when he died. Mathematicians looked for a proof for three centuries without success, although many people were convinced that a relatively simple proof could be found. (Proofs of special cases were found, such as the proof of the case when n = 3 by Euler and the proof of the n = 4 case by Fermat himself.) Over the years, several established mathematicians thought that they had proved this theorem. In the nineteenth century, one of these failed",
