        "rawText": "EXAMPLE 17 What do the statements ∀x< 0( x2 > 0), ∀y≠ 0( y3 ≠ 0), and ∃z> 0( z2 = 2) mean, where the\ndomain in each case consists of the real numbers?\nSolution: The statement∀x< 0( x2 > 0) states that for every real numberx with x < 0, x2 > 0.\nThat is, it states “The square of a negative real number is positive.” This statement is the same\nas∀x(x < 0 → x2 > 0).\nThe statement∀y≠ 0( y3 ≠ 0) states that for every real number y with y ≠ 0, we have y3 ≠\n0. That is, it states “The cube of every nonzero real number is nonzero.” This statement is\nequivalent to∀y(y≠ 0 → y\n3 ≠ 0).\nFinally, the statement∃z> 0( z2 = 2) states that there exists a real numberz with z > 0 such\nthat z2 = 2. That is, it states “There is a positive square root of 2.” This statement is equivalent\nto∃z(z> 0∧z2 = 2). ◂",
        "text": "EXAMPLE 17 What do the statements ∀x< 0( x2 > 0), ∀y≠ 0( y3 ≠ 0), and ∃z> 0( z2 = 2) mean, where the domain in each case consists of the real numbers? Solution: The statement∀x< 0( x2 > 0) states that for every real numberx with x < 0, x2 > 0. That is, it states “The square of a negative real number is positive.” This statement is the same as∀x(x < 0 → x2 > 0). The statement∀y≠ 0( y3 ≠ 0) states that for every real number y with y ≠ 0, we have y3 ≠ 0. That is, it states “The cube of every nonzero real number is nonzero.” This statement is equivalent to∀y(y≠ 0 → y 3 ≠ 0). Finally, the statement∃z> 0( z2 = 2) states that there exists a real numberz with z > 0 such that z2 = 2. That is, it states “There is a positive square root of 2.” This statement is equivalent to∃z(z> 0∧z2 = 2). ◂",
