        "rawText": "EXAMPLE 14 Express the negation of the statement ∀x∃y(xy= 1) so that no negation precedes a quantiﬁer.\nSolution: By successively applying De Morgan’s laws for quantiﬁers in Table 2 of\nSection 1.4, we can move the negation in¬∀x∃y(xy= 1) inside all the quantiﬁers. We ﬁnd that\nExtra \nExamples\n¬∀x∃y(xy= 1) is equivalent to∃x¬∃y(xy= 1), which is equivalent to∃x∀y¬(xy= 1). Because\n¬(xy = 1) can be expressed more simply asxy ≠ 1, we conclude that our negated statement can\nbe expressed as∃x∀y(xy≠ 1). ◂",
        "text": "EXAMPLE 14 Express the negation of the statement ∀x∃y(xy= 1) so that no negation precedes a quantifier. Solution: By successively applying De Morgan’s laws for quantifiers in Table 2 of Section 1.4, we can move the negation in¬∀x∃y(xy= 1) inside all the quantifiers. We find that Extra Examples ¬∀x∃y(xy= 1) is equivalent to∃x¬∃y(xy= 1), which is equivalent to∃x∀y¬(xy= 1). Because ¬(xy = 1) can be expressed more simply asxy ≠ 1, we conclude that our negated statement can be expressed as∃x∀y(xy≠ 1). ◂",
