        "kind": "example",
        "section": "1.7",
        "subsection": "1.7.8",
        "rawText": "EXAMPLE 17 What is wrong with this “proof”?\n“Theorem”: If n2 is positive, then n is positive.\n“Proof ”: Suppose that n2 is positive. Because the conditional statement “If n is positive, then\nn2 is positive” is true, we can conclude that n is positive.\nSolution: Let P(n)b e“ n is positive” and Q(n)b e“ n2 is positive.” Then our hypothesis is Q(n).\nThe statement “If n is positive, then n2 is positive” is the statement ∀n(P(n)→ Q(n)). From\nthe hypothesis Q(n) and the statement ∀n(P(n)→ Q(n)) we cannot conclude P(n), because we\nare not using a valid rule of inference. Instead, this is an example of the fallacy of aﬃrming\nthe conclusion. A counterexample is supplied by n =−1 for which n2 = 1 is positive, but n is\nnegative. ◂",
        "text": "EXAMPLE 17 What is wrong with this “proof”? “Theorem”: If n2 is positive, then n is positive. “Proof ”: Suppose that n2 is positive. Because the conditional statement “If n is positive, then n2 is positive” is true, we can conclude that n is positive. Solution: Let P(n)b e“ n is positive” and Q(n)b e“ n2 is positive.” Then our hypothesis is Q(n). The statement “If n is positive, then n2 is positive” is the statement ∀n(P(n)→ Q(n)). From the hypothesis Q(n) and the statement ∀n(P(n)→ Q(n)) we cannot conclude P(n), because we are not using a valid rule of inference. Instead, this is an example of the fallacy of affirming the conclusion. A counterexample is supplied by n =−1 for which n2 = 1 is positive, but n is negative. ◂",
