        "solutionRaw": "The problem with this “proof” is that we missed the case of x = 0. When x = 0,\nx2 = 0 is not positive, so the supposed theorem is false. If p is “x is a real number,” then\nwe can prove results where p is the hypothesis with three cases, p1, p2, and p3, where\np1 is “x is positive,” p2 is “x is negative,” and p3 is “x = 0” because of the equivalence\np ↔ p1 ∨p2 ∨p3. ◂",
        "solution": "The problem with this “proof” is that we missed the case of x = 0. When x = 0, x2 = 0 is not positive, so the supposed theorem is false. If p is “x is a real number,” then we can prove results where p is the hypothesis with three cases, p1, p2, and p3, where p1 is “x is positive,” p2 is “x is negative,” and p3 is “x = 0” because of the equivalence p ↔ p1 ∨p2 ∨p3. ◂",
        "solutionLine": 6115,
        "terminator": "◂"
