        "subsection": "1.8.4",
        "rawText": "EXAMPLE 13 Show that if a and b are real numbers and a ≠ 0, then there is a unique real number r such that\nar+ b = 0.\n104 1 / The Foundations: Logic and Proofs\nSolution: First, note that the real number r =−b∕a is a solution of ar+ b = 0 because\na(−b∕a)+ b =−b+ b = 0. Consequently, a real number r exists for which ar+ b = 0. This\nis the existence part of the proof.\nSecond, suppose that s is a real number such that as+ b = 0. Then ar+ b = as+ b, where\nr =−b∕a. Subtracting b from both sides, we ﬁnd that ar = as. Dividing both sides of this lastExtra \nExamples equation by a, which is nonzero, we see that r = s. This establishes the uniqueness part of the\nproof. ◂",
        "text": "EXAMPLE 13 Show that if a and b are real numbers and a ≠ 0, then there is a unique real number r such that ar+ b = 0. Solution: First, note that the real number r =−b∕a is a solution of ar+ b = 0 because a(−b∕a)+ b =−b+ b = 0. Consequently, a real number r exists for which ar+ b = 0. This is the existence part of the proof. Second, suppose that s is a real number such that as+ b = 0. Then ar+ b = as+ b, where r =−b∕a. Subtracting b from both sides, we find that ar = as. Dividing both sides of this lastExtra Examples equation by a, which is nonzero, we see that r = s. This establishes the uniqueness part of the proof. ◂",
