        "section": "1.8",
        "subsection": "1.8.8",
        "rawText": "EXAMPLE 21 Can you use straight triominoes to tile a standard checkerboard?\nSolution: The standard checkerboard contains 64 squares and each triomino covers three\nsquares. Consequently, if triominoes tile a board, the number of squares of the board must be\na multiple of 3. Because 64 is not a multiple of 3, triominoes cannot be used to cover an 8 × 8\ncheckerboard. ◂",
        "text": "EXAMPLE 21 Can you use straight triominoes to tile a standard checkerboard? Solution: The standard checkerboard contains 64 squares and each triomino covers three squares. Consequently, if triominoes tile a board, the number of squares of the board must be a multiple of 3. Because 64 is not a multiple of 3, triominoes cannot be used to cover an 8 × 8 checkerboard. ◂",
