        "kind": "definition",
        "section": "1.3",
        "subsection": "1.3.1",
        "rawText": "Deﬁnition 1 A compound proposition that is always true, no matter what the truth values of the proposi-\ntional variables that occur in it, is called atautology. A compound proposition that is always\nfalse is called acontradiction. A compound proposition that is neither a tautology nor a con-\ntradiction is called a contingency.",
        "text": "Definition 1 A compound proposition that is always true, no matter what the truth values of the propositional variables that occur in it, is called atautology. A compound proposition that is always false is called acontradiction. A compound proposition that is neither a tautology nor a contradiction is called a contingency.",
